🔥 RULE 1 — “If UI can change → it MUST come from state.”

    Examples:

    Text that updates
    Checkbox
    Switch
    Slider
    Selected item
    Login input
    Dark/light theme
    Timer
    Counte
    List items
    📌 If something can change → use state.
    If it will never change → NO state.


    Does UI value change?
        |
        |--- NO ---> Use normal variable / constant (no state)
        |
        |--- YES ---> Who controls the value?
                        |
                        |--- Only this composable --> remember { mutableStateOf() }
                        |
                        |--- Parent controls ----------> Hoist state (value + onChange)
                        |
                        |--- Screen-level logic -----> ViewModel + StateFlow



⭐ The 4 most-used patterns you must learn (only these)

    1️⃣ State inside composable (UI-local)
    var text by remember { mutableStateOf("") }


    Use when:

    Local UI behavior

    Won’t survive rotation

    Only that composable uses it

    2️⃣ State that survives rotation
    var text by rememberSaveable { mutableStateOf("") }


    Use for:

    Form input

    Selected tab

    Login fields

    3️⃣ Hoisted state (parent controls)

    Child:

    @Composable
    fun NameField(value: String, onValueChange: (String) -> Unit) {
        TextField(value = value, onValueChange = onValueChange)
    }


    Parent:

    var name by remember { mutableStateOf("") }
    NameField(name) { name = it }


    Use when:

    Making reusable components

    Clean UI architecture

    4️⃣ ViewModel state (screen-level)

    ViewModel:

    val name = MutableStateFlow("")
    fun updateName(newName: String) { name.value = newName }


    Composable:

    val name by vm.name.collectAsState()


    Use when:

    Full screen

    Data from repo/DB

    Must survive process death

    App-level logic